data:
10/10 masterpiece story wise and art design;
10/10 faithful adaptation to the original Hellraiser franchise (1-4th installment);
10/10 gameplay loop;
8/10 combat system;

You HAVE to play it if you are a horror game, especially psychological horror game fan. I would classify this game as Horror Erotica genre if you want more detail about it without any spoilers. 

Let me elaborate:
1. The game does NOT shove modern political agendas down player's, a rare quality that alone warrants a purchase;
2. Rather than buying a unique IP just to butcher it into something unrecognizable, the studio writer actually took the time to comb through Clive Barker’s source material and secured Barker's personal approval before making the game;
3. The game's highlight is the masterful design of the labyrinth in Hell. It instills a constant sense of urgency, surreal disorientation, and pressure to escape, yet players cannot help but stop and be drawn deeper into it;
4. If I have to nitpick this masterpiece, the combat system, especially the final boss encounter, is poorly designed. Combat system is bit janky, slow, and less intuitive comparing to other more polished titles. Also, unlike any other RPG games that dynamically scale loots based on player's inventory, this game offers only a finite pool of resources throughout the campaign, meaning if players will face an unavoidable soft-lock if you are out of ammo during the final Boss fight;
